-### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Interpolation Error:** Standard vertex attribute interpolation fails for MCOP because the projection cannot be recast into a traditional homogeneous matrix, and long straight lines appear curved [S1]. The proxy geometry and vertex-shader projection solve this [S1].
-*   **Tile Misalignment:** If the object moves too slowly relative to the display refresh rate, misalignment between tiles becomes visible because the 96 display projectors are updated in batches of 16 [S1].
-*   **Ray Length:** If the clipped ray length is less than epsilon, the fragment is discarded as it lies outside the volume bounds [S1].
+**Parameters:**
+*   Proxy Tessellation: 8x8 quads [S1].
+*   Volume Texture Format: Single 4-component texture (RGB: precomputed gradient, A: voxel value) [S1].
+*   Lookup Table: 1D RGBA texture [S1].
+*   $\mathbf{E}_w$: Projector position [S1].
+*   $V_d$: Virtual observer distance [S1].
+*   $V_h$: Virtual observer height [S1].
+*   $\log_2 V$: Log2 of voxel size [S1].
+*   $DL$: Step size [S1].
+*   $S_0$: Screen pixel size [S1].
+*   $\tan \Phi$: Angular spread factor [S1].
+
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Vertex Attribute Interpolation Error:** Standard vertex attribute interpolation does not produce correct per-pixel ray parameter values for MCOP. This is handled by using a proxy geometry and computing projections in the vertex shader [S1].
+*   **Tile Misalignment Artifacts:** When objects are moved with a too slow refresh rate, misalignment between tiles can become visible because the 96 display projectors are updated in batches of 16 [S1].
+*   **Out of Bounds:** If ray length is less than epsilon, discard fragment as it is out of bounds [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Performance:** The method requires millions of rays to be propagated for hundreds of steps at full resolution, which is the main limiting factor for interactive frame rates [S1].
-*   **Optimization:** To improve performance, the sampling rate is lowered during interaction by reducing pixel count (e.g., 2.5mm precision on screen) and doubling the integration step size [S1].
-*   **Memory:** The volume is stored in a single 4-component texture, and a 1D LUT is used for the transfer function [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Provides a fully continuous perceived image with correct parallax and accommodation cues, unlike discrete multiview systems [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Performance is constrained by single GPU volume rendering capabilities; heavy optimization (lowering resolution/step size) is required for interactivity [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The method requires propagating millions of rays for hundreds of steps through the volume at full resolution (1 ray/pixel and 1 sample/voxel) [S1]. To improve interactive frame rates, the sampling rate is lowered during interaction by reducing pixel count (2.5mm precision on screen) and doubling the integration step-size [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** The volume is stored in a single 4-component texture with a Mipmap pyramid [S1]. An additional 1D RGBA texture is used for the lookup table [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Provides a fully continuous perceived image, unlike other contemporary multiview technologies that force users into fixed positions due to abrupt view-image changes when crossing discrete viewing zones [S1]. Users can exploit accommodation, stereo, and motion parallax to gain understanding of complex shapes [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Performance is limited by single GPU volume renderer performance at full resolution [S1]. Tile misalignment artifacts can occur with slow refresh rates [S1].
+*   **When to use:** Interactive light field displays requiring continuous viewing zones and multi-user capability [S1].
+*   **When not to use:** When high-resolution static rendering without interaction is required, or when the display hardware does not support the specific MCOP projection model [S1].
 
 ## Background
-This method was introduced by Agus et al. in 2008 [S1]. It was developed for an interactive light field display system, aiming to provide multi-user 3D volume rendering with continuous viewing zones. The work builds upon GPU-accelerated volume rendering techniques and addresses the specific geometric challenges of projecting volume data onto a multi-projector light field display.
+*   **Origin:** The method was introduced by Agus et al. in 2008 [S1].
+*   **History:** It was developed to address the limitations of traditional multiview technologies on light field displays, specifically the issue of discrete viewing zones [S1].
+*   **Usage:** Used for rendering datasets like Chameleon, Visible Human head, and rotational angiography on interactive light field displays [S1].
 
